# Multi-Shelled Hollow Metal-Organic Frameworks

## abstract
Hollow metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are promising materials with sophisticated structures, such as multiple shells, that cannot only enhance the properties of MOFs but also endow them with new functions. Herein, we show a rational strategy to fabricate multi-shelled hollow chromium (III) terephthalate MOFs (MIL-101) with single-crystalline shells through step-by-step crystal growth and subsequent etching processes. This strategy relies on the creation of inhomogeneous MOF crystals in which the outer layer is chemically more robust than the inner layer and can be selectively etched by acetic acid. The regulation of MOF nucleation and crystallization allows the tailoring of the cavity size and shell thickness of each layer. The resultant multi-shelled hollow MIL-101 crystals show significantly enhanced catalytic activity during styrene oxidation. The insight gained from this systematic study will aid in the rational design and synthesis of other multi-shelled hollow structures and the further expansion of their applications.

Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs), which are assembled by metal centers/clusters and functional organic ligands, constitute one of the most fascinating research fields of the recent decades.[1] MOFs possess uniform and tunable microporous structures with large surface areas, which are promising for a variety of applications, including gas sorption,[2] separations,[3] catalysis,[4] and drug delivery.[5] The tailoring of their shapes, sizes, and pore structures with a rational design and controllability, though crucial for further applications, remains a challenge for practical preparations. Great efforts have been made to fabricate MOFs with exquisitely detailed nanostructures to enhance performance or explore new properties.[6] In particular, given the development of various inorganic hollow nanostructures and their promising applications,[7] hollow MOFs containing large enclosed cavities and thin shells have attracted wide interest because the enclosed space is expected to be useful for chemical storage and compartmentation in applications such as catalysis, drug delivery and energy storage.[8] For example, De Vos and coworkers reported an interfacial synthesis method for the fabrication of hollow MOF capsules with selective permeability to effectively entrap guest species.[8b] Wang and coworkers loaded a noble-metal catalyst into the hollow MOF to enhance its catalytic activity.[9] Nevertheless, current methods generally address the production of hollow MOFs with polycrystalline shells, which involves the assembly of small MOF crystals.[8c,d,g,10] Recently, the research group of Tsung developed a double-solvent mediated overgrowth strategy to generate a hollow zeolitic imidazolate framework-8 (ZIF-8) with a single-crystalline structure, which is considered to be critical for improved performance in MOF applications.[11] However, relative to the various hollow structures of inorganic nanomaterials,[7] the construction of hollow MOFs with sophisticated structures such as multishelled MOFs remains a considerable challenge.
Given that it is synthetically challenging to remove the intermediate layer in multilayer nanoparticles (NPs) during the fabrication of most multi-shelled NPs described in the literature, the creation of inhomogeneous NPs, in which the outer layer of the shell is chemically more robust than the inner layer, is an effective alternative method.[7a] For example, Chen and co-workers have demonstrated that the silica shell on NPs formed by a typical Stober method was inhomogeneous in nature and could be used to synthesize multi-shelled $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ .[7a] A chemical etching method, also developed by the research group of Yamauchi, was used to synthesize hollow Prussian-blue (PB) analogs in view of location-related chemical stability differences.[12] However, since the MOF crystals are generally homogeneous in nature, engineering them with different compositions or stability is critically important for selective etching. Herein, we demonstrate a rational method to fabricate a single-crystalline multishelled hollow chromium (III) terephthalate MOF (MIL-101) through step-by-step crystal growth and subsequent etching processes. Inhomogeneous MIL-101 crystals were fabricated by a hydrothermal reaction using 1,4-benzene dicarboxylic acid $(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC})$ as the organic linker and chromium ions as metallic node. Thus, we can choose conditions to directly and selectively etch the inner, less stable section to achieve a hollow motif (Scheme 1). Impressively, the use of these inhomogeneous MOF crystals as templates to repeat the growth-etching process for a second or third time has enabled the successful preparation of sophisticated multi-shelled hollow MOFs (MSHMs). The cavity size and the shell thickness of each layer in the MSHMs can be regulated through careful control of the MOF nucleation and crystallization processes. These MSHMs exhibit properties distinct from those of solid MOFs without hollow interiors. When evaluated as a catalyst, the as-synthesized MSHMs manifested higher catalytic activity. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first example of the construction of multi-shelled hollow MOFs as catalyst.
Scheme 1 illustrates the structural formation processes of the MSHMs through step-by-step crystal growth and subsequent etching processes. Specifically, chromium (III) terephthalate MOF (MIL-101) was chosen due to its high thermal and chemical stability and large cavities (ca. $3.4~\mathrm{{nm}}$ ) that enable mass transport through pentagonal and hexagonal windows.[13] The hydrothermal reaction of $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}9\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $\mathrm{\bf{H}}_{2}\mathrm{\bf{B}\mathrm{{DC}}}$ resulted in MIL-101 crystals with a truncated octahedral shape and an average size of about $200\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure 1a). When treated with acetic acid aqueous solution at $180^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ , the inner crystallites were preferentially etched, which enabled the formation of hollow cavities inside the MOF crystals (Scheme 1b). The morphology and average particle size of the resultant hollow MIL-101 crystals were not significantly changed after the etching process, as shown in Figure 1 c. Each crystal possessed a sphere-shaped interior hollow cavity. The average cavity size and shell thickness were approximately 110 and $45\mathrm{nm}$ , respectively. The singlecrystalline structure was confirmed by a high-resolution transmission electron microscopy (HRTEM) image and the corresponding fast Fourier transform (FFT) pattern (Figure 1d and the inset). To illustrate the inhomogeneity of the MOF crystals by location, we denoted the easily etchable inner section as the “less stable” part and the remaining section as the “more stable” part, and abbreviated as “LS” and “MS” respectively, as shown in Scheme 1a. Therefore, the hydrothermally synthesized MIL-101 crystals are considered to have a “LS@MS” structure, which is similar to a core–shell structure. When using these initial $\mathbf{\partial}^{\cdot}\mathbf{LS}@\mathbf{MS}^{,\prime}$ MIL-101 particles as template to react with additional chromium ions and a $\mathrm{\bfH}_{2}\mathrm{\bfBDC}$ aqueous solution for another $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ , an additional layer of MIL-101 epitaxially grew (Scheme 1c), with the truncated octahedral particle size increasing to $350\mathrm{nm}$ (see Figure S1 a in the Supporting Information). As expected, the newly formed MOF layer also possessed a $\mathbf{\nabla}^{\mathsf{L S}(\varpi\mathbf{M}\mathbf{S}^{\prime})}$ core– shell structure. When these particles were immersed into an acetic acid aqueous solution for $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ , a double-shelled hollow MOF (DSHM) was obtained (Scheme 1d). As shown in Figure 1 e and 1 f, the shell thickness of each layer within the same MSHM sample was similar with an approximate width of $50\mathrm{nm}$ . The shell diameters of DSHM inner and outer cavity were 90 and $250\mathrm{nm}$ , respectively. Both the outer and inner shells of the MSHMs share the same crystallographic orientation, as suggested by the FFT patterns of the whole particles (insets of Figure 1 f,h). The slight mismatch of in the FFT points of the DSHM may be due to a shift of the inner hollow particle after the formation of the hollow structure. Repeating the growth process three or more times with subsequent etching enabled the fabrication of triple-shelled (TSHM) or multi-shelled hollow MIL-101 particles (Scheme 1 e,f, Figure 1 g,h). Powder X-ray diffraction (XRD) confirmed the crystallographic structure and phase purity of the resulting MSHMs. As shown in Figure S2, the positions and relative intensities of the diffraction peaks of the MSHMs are similar to those of the solid crystals and consistent with the simulated result. No additional diffraction peaks from impurities were detected, which indicates the high purity of the product.
The formation of the hollow structures relies on the inhomogeneous nature of the as-prepared MIL-101 crystals, which can be understood by a surface-energy-driven mechanism.[9a] Generally, the typical synthesis of nanocrystals can be divided into three stages: 1) nucleation, 2) evolution of nuclei into seeds, and 3) growth of seeds into nanocrystals.[14]
In the initial stage of the synthetic process, the concentration of the solute is much higher than the critical nucleation point, thus explosive nucleation could take place. Specifically, a large number of small coordination polymer units nucleate from the solution and quickly aggregate to minimize the surface energy. Afterwards, as the concentration of metal ions and organic ligands quickly drops below the minimum supersaturation level, no additional nucleation occurs, which finally leads to the formation of MOF crystals with low surface energy on the surface of the aggregates of small particles.[8a,9a,12,15] Thus, the inside core of each MOF particle has more defects than the outside shell and the outer layer of the crystal is considered chemically more robust than the inner layer (Scheme 1a). This inhomogeneous nature of MOF particles at different locations enables the inner crystallites to be preferentially etched by acetic acid into molecules and/or ions, which can diffuse into the dispersion through the pores of the MOF (Figure S3). This process can be easily understood with regard to the “molecular mechanisms” when considering the coordination bond between the metal ion and the ligand. The unsaturated coordinated electrophilic chromium atoms in the defective part are more accessible to the nucleophilic oxygen atoms in water. Meanwhile, hydrogen binds to oxygen on the ligand, leading to the collapse of the crystal in the inner part of MIL-101.[16] This mechanism was verified by a series of control experiments. Firstly, sample formed at the early growth stage was taken to investigate. The near spherical morphology and weak intensity of XRD peaks confirms its polycrystalline nature (Figure S4). Furthermore, we found that the hollow MOF can also be obtained in deionized water and diluted hydrochloric acid, but not in acetone or acetone solution of sodium acetate (Figure S5), which indicates that the H cation and not the acetic acid anion is the key in the formation of hollow MOFs. The proposed mechanism suggests that the stability of the MOF can be controlled by tuning the nucleation and growth rate of MOFs. Typically, the MIL-101 crystals obtained under varying reactant concentrations have different stabilities upon acid etching. If these $\mathbf{\partial}^{\cdot}\mathbf{LS}@\mathbf{MS}^{,\prime}$ structured MIL-101 particles are added to an additional high-concentration reactant solution, then the newly formed layer of MIL-101 also contains the inhomogeneous “LS@MS” core–shell structure, which makes the fabrication of multi-shelled hollow particles possible. Uniform “MS” MIL-101 (MIL-101-HAc) can be obtained by adding acetic acid $(0.5\mathrm{mL})$ into the system to significantly reduce their nucleation rates (Figure S6).
Hollow MOFs consist of hollow cavities and microporous shells. The precise control of the cavity size and porous shell thickness can enhance their intrinsic properties and has significance for the practical applications of hollow structured MOFs. For example, the cavities within the porous shells can be used as nanoreactors for catalytically loading active species for catalytic reactions[17] or nanocontainers for drug storage.[18] The single-crystalline microporous shells with welltuned thicknesses are a highly favored pathway for the mass transfer of reactants and products in and out of the voids.[7d] Thus, the regulation of pore size and shell thickness is of great importance. Here, we demonstrate that the cavity size of MSHMs can be well controlled by tuning the etching time (Figure 2a). Figure $2\mathsf{b-d}$ shows three representative SSHM samples after 1, 2, and $6\mathrm{{h}}$ etching processes. A small interior cavity of about $40\mathrm{nm}$ was formed in the center of the MIL101 particles at $^{\textrm{1h}}$ (Figure 2b). The cavity became larger with an increase in the etching time: approximately 90 and $140\mathrm{nm}$ after 2 and $6\mathrm{n}$ , respectively (Figure 2 c,d). Similarly, the DSHM cavity size can also be well controlled by tuning the etching time, during which the shell thickness gradually decreased from 70 to $50\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure 2e–g).
To demonstrate the structural advantage of the MSHMs, the oxidation of styrene, an important industrial process, was chosen as a probe reaction (Figure 4 a).[19] Figure 4 b shows the conversion and selectivity for the oxidation of styrene on solid MIL-101, SSHM, DSHM, and TSHM crystals. The hollow-structured MIL-101 exhibited much higher activity than the solid MIL-101 crystal, and the catalytic activity increased with the number of layers in the MSHMs. The conversion of styrene reached $90\%$ after $12\mathrm{{h}}$ for the TSHM. In comparison, an $18\%$ conversion occurred using solid MIL101. The specific rate of conversion of the TSHM was five times greater than that of solid MIL-101 at $12\mathrm{h}$ , which indicates that the multi-shelled structure can significantly improve the catalytic activity. In addition, the MSHMs exhibited an improved benzaldehyde selectivity in comparison to solid MIL-101. To further investigate the origin of the performance enhancement, the BET surface areas of the MSHMs were measured by ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption–desorption isotherms, and the results were 2847, 2040, 1776, and $1486\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for solid MIL-101, SSHM, DSHM, and TSHM, respectively. The decrease in the BET surface area of the hollow MIL-101 might be caused by the formation of cavities, and thus the porous structure of the materials was slightly destroyed in the etching process.[20] The decreasing trend in the surface area is opposite to that of the catalytic activity of the materials. Thus, we suggest that the enhanced catalytic performance of the MSHMs may be ascribed to the construction of multi-shelled hollow structures, in which the active site in each layer can be used more efficiently. In addition, the hollow interiors between each layer may improve the adsorption of the substrate in heterogeneous catalysis.[7b,d,9a,21] The catalytic stability is of great importance for practical applications of highly efficient catalysts. To address this concern, recycling experiments were performed with the TSHM using styrene as the substrate. After each reaction, the catalyst was centrifuged, washed thoroughly with acetonitrile and ethanol, dried under vacuum, and reused for the subsequent cycles. The catalyst showed no significant changes in terms of the catalytic activity and selectivity after successive reuses of up to five cycles (Figure 4c). The phase and morphology of the reused catalyst remained virtually unchanged relative to that of the fresh catalyst (Figure S8), which also verified the stability of the catalyst.
In conclusion, we have developed a facile method to fabricate single-crystalline multi-shelled hollow MIL-101 crystals through step-by-step crystal growth and subsequent etching processes. The cavity size and shell thickness were precisely controlled by regulating the etching time and the reactant concentration for the synthesis of each layer. The catalytic oxidation of styrene indicates that the design of the multi-shelled structure contributes to its excellent catalytic performance relative to that of the solid nanocrystals. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first example of the construction of a catalytic system based on multi-shelled hollow MOFs. This work may reveal new opportunities for preparing advanced materials based on various complex multi-shelled hollow MOF structures with multifunctional applications.
